1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:09,440
Agents I found are bad at making changes and are good at doing it one shot. I go as huge as possible. Do you look at the output? Do you review the code?

2
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:19,520
Oh yeah, and I throw it away if I don't like it. If I don't understand it, I throw it away. This is Carlos Kelbahm, one of the best engineers I know. His main agent doesn't even touch files.

3
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:28,000
Wow, it's focused on only one thing. How am I ever going to get a good answer out of this thing if the only thing it looks at is, can I write the code for you? Right now.

4
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:39,520
So I just forced it to never do that again. They are good at writing code, but not at engineering an application. I think we need to get rid of the heap. The heap just needs to go completely.

5
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:50,560
We don't need pointers, we don't need dynamic memory. If for some reason AI disappears, everything will fall down. Do you think that will happen? I see three things happening.

6
00:00:53,360 --> 00:01:00,324
What is the single biggest thing that you would say engineering has really shifted towards, which is different than a few years ago?

7
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:12,160
So, first, the thing that I'm seeing that I don't really like, and that is that I've seen that cooperation and coordination is starting to slip away.

8
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:23,520
So the importance of pull requests, people working together, commenting on their own pull requests, on other people's pull requests. some people are even saying a pull request might disappear.

9
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,028
And what I'm seeing is a more individual way of developing and less a cooperation.

10
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:34,212
You work together with your agents, but you don't work together with your team anymore.

11
00:01:34,702 --> 00:01:38,080
Or at least that's the trend I'm seeing, and I think it's a dangerous trend.

12
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:40,400
Why do you think that is specifically?

13
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:48,240
And for me, I have the assumption that in practice it might be what we see online, kind of on Reddit, that's my bubble or YouTube,

14
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,000
because there are a lot of solo developers that are kind of on the frontier of things.

15
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:59,844
But I don't know if that resonates what happens in bigger organizations where there are still bigger teams and people collaborating with agents and each other.

16
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:04,480
Yeah, so I have the privilege of not being on any social media.

17
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:10,320
I don't have a Reddit account. I mean, I only use LinkedIn to talk to you.

18
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:21,760
I have absolutely zero interest in following social media, but I do read a lot and I read articles and I read white papers and I

19
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:35,360
talk to a lot of companies and a lot of people in companies. And so there's a difference between building something fast and not finishing it, and building something that you hope will last.

20
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:36,800
And so

21
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:46,800
it's that classic marathon versus sprint thing, and people building things by themselves usually don't push it that extra edge

22
00:02:47,182 --> 00:02:48,480
towards being completed.

23
00:02:49,600 --> 00:03:00,480
And that is, I think, what the difference is: showing something on social media or on YouTube. Like, for example, you see a lot of people building games, uh, very fast.

24
00:03:00,974 --> 00:03:09,840
And the reason why I want to do air quotes is because the biggest problem with games is listening to your users, doing the game design correctly,

25
00:03:10,062 --> 00:03:15,280
and that is still something; yeah, you can iterate with your agent, but you need to communicate to the world.

26
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:24,480
So I think people working within their bubble, not having to communicate with other people, but always was one of the most difficult things to do.

27
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:28,080
I think that's only being enforced now because you can

28
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:31,440
you can show a lot of progress,

29
00:03:32,366 --> 00:03:33,920
but you do that by yourself.

30
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:35,600
And

31
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:43,200
remembering why you're building a piece of software or for whom you're building it, that's why I said product is becoming more important.

32
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,932
I think that is the thing that is really taking a beating right now.

33
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:53,120
Do you have any ideas on how to solve this?

34
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:55,920
Because I think it's very easy.

35
00:03:56,110 --> 00:03:57,280
I'm in my little bubble.

36
00:03:57,582 --> 00:04:00,960
I used to think starting things is one of the hardest things. that was it for me.

37
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:07,280
Now I've started many things and I've never finished it. People used to tell me that, and I was like, I don't get it. I only start when I'm able to finish it.

38
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:09,920
Now starting has never become this easy for me.

39
00:04:10,702 --> 00:04:12,480
Even with my license, I have credits.

40
00:04:12,686 --> 00:04:19,600
I'm like, oh, I have this little bar, and at the end of the month, I want my bar at the end of the week, I want my bar to be as close to 100% as I can.

41
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,956
So before I go to bed, I'm like, what can I build?

42
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:23,600
A game. I've definitely built games.

43
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,720
I built a harness that builds games so other people can build games.

44
00:04:27,150 --> 00:04:28,292
It's like one meta layer.

45
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:33,988
I'm trying to solve problems that I think are gonna happen in the future and seeing kind of what the results are of those.

46
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:40,960
But then I don't take the extra mile, I don't harden it, I don't finish it, I don't build this out to become a product that I then sell.

47
00:04:41,198 --> 00:04:43,076
I start many things, I don't finish many things.

48
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:43,620
Yeah.

49
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:46,640
But that that was always the case.

50
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,240
So I have built

51
00:04:50,030 --> 00:04:52,516
Gosh, five compilers.

52
00:04:53,134 --> 00:04:54,480
I've started them all.

53
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:57,380
They all were almost finished.

54
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:01,028
And there's only one I can say was actually finished.

55
00:05:01,774 --> 00:05:11,040
And so starting things and not finishing them was always a part of software development because you have a cool idea, you want to learn, you start building.

56
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:20,800
The only difference now is why are you starting the project? Are you starting the project to learn or to experiment with the agents or to actually build a product?

57
00:05:21,102 --> 00:05:26,240
So I think the intention behind what you're going to be doing in the future needs to harden.

58
00:05:26,510 --> 00:05:32,640
And as soon as that intention hardens, that question you just asked goes away a little bit.

59
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:37,280
So if the if the if the intention is to just have fun,

60
00:05:38,254 --> 00:05:39,972
man, you've already reached your goal.

61
00:05:40,654 --> 00:05:44,400
If the intention is to build a company out of it, then you need to finish something.

62
00:05:45,422 --> 00:05:54,320
And then for people that are experiencing this, me and my agent were kind of in a bubble. And I if I ask a if I have a question instead of going and reaching out to you, I would ask my agent.

63
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:57,348
That's kind of the way of working that I see, at least in my environment.

64
00:05:57,902 --> 00:06:01,348
What would your advice be for people to step out of that and start collaborating more?

65
00:06:06,734 --> 00:06:10,160
To be really honest, I've been thinking about this for a very long time.

66
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:17,252
And I think the the way we work together and the way we build software has to change.

67
00:06:17,646 --> 00:06:20,900
So you've had this open source movement for a very long time.

68
00:06:21,614 --> 00:06:29,920
I think we need to have focus more on things like uh meetups, uh sharing information,

69
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:37,440
sharing knowledge, working together, have groups that want to review each other's products.

70
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,044
I mean, we need to

71
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:42,884
really come together and work together.

72
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:54,240
and that means that even though it is easier to stay in your own bubble, I think we need to find a way how to lower that bar of

73
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:58,660
finding people who are just curious about what you're doing.

74
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:08,960
And that also means we need to be a little bit more open and not so afraid to give our ideas away, because ideas, ideas are people always say that ideas are cheap,

75
00:07:09,198 --> 00:07:11,748
but they don't feel cheap to the person having the idea.

76
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:17,732
They are cheap in the global scheme of things, but they feel personal.

77
00:07:18,062 --> 00:07:24,720
So sharing an idea and that that fear that somebody else is gonna run away with it or or so or you did something wrong or stuff like that.

78
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:29,200
I think that is the thing we need to focus on by building a very

79
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:31,716
inclusive community.

80
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:33,680
And

81
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:41,060
so the coolest example I can give is I don't know if you know World of Warcraft Forever that just came out.

82
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:47,940
I mean, when you go into the beta and you just ask a silly question, how do I turn off this setting? You get four or five people helping you.

83
00:07:48,494 --> 00:07:52,804
That's the feeling we need to have, but then for people who want to build software, yeah.

84
00:07:53,358 --> 00:07:55,840
Yeah, the trend I see is that you would ask your agent, right?

85
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:03,840
There's no reason to ask a human, because also some people don't have patience in general for people asking questions which they think are silly.

86
00:08:04,206 --> 00:08:07,200
I've heard people say, ask your agent first before asking me, which is

87
00:08:08,046 --> 00:08:10,640
it's kind of funny in a very horrible way.

88
00:08:13,198 --> 00:08:14,560
but that is what people are doing now.

89
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:18,560
They're more in isolation. Imposter syndrome has never been a bigger thing, I feel like.

90
00:08:19,630 --> 00:08:27,600
And people rely on their agents to give them guidance, even though they might be steered in the wrong direction or they might not see kind of the bigger picture, or even from a learning perspective,

91
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:31,600
might not learn as much when interacting with agents instead of humans.

92
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:32,160
Yeah.

93
00:08:32,462 --> 00:08:33,200
I mean, humans,

94
00:08:35,758 --> 00:08:41,060
it is slower to ask a human because you will not get an immediate answer.

95
00:08:42,414 --> 00:08:46,400
but agents tend to want to give you the answer you want to hear.

96
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:51,680
Uh, you can, you can solve a little bit of that by having multiple models.

97
00:08:51,982 --> 00:08:59,600
uh, you can talk to and then challenge each other, and you can do some harness things like adversarial setups. and so you can do things.

98
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:03,040
but what happens is that um,

99
00:09:06,222 --> 00:09:11,904
you will only start to communicate with these machines that are very fast to answer, and

100
00:09:12,814 --> 00:09:18,784
you will not take the time to sit by yourself with your own thoughts.

101
00:09:19,118 --> 00:09:27,584
Just let the chaos of the world go through your head until you find a little bit of clarity that you can use to have a good idea

102
00:09:27,744 --> 00:09:30,212
or to know how to do things differently.

103
00:09:30,606 --> 00:09:33,988
I mean, we can go into how I write software with agents as well.

104
00:09:34,464 --> 00:09:44,944
But I think that that original idea of creativity, like John Clee said, the open and the closed creativity, the open one needs a state,

105
00:09:45,104 --> 00:09:46,788
a mental state of being open.

106
00:09:47,278 --> 00:09:49,188
And you only get that by

107
00:09:50,510 --> 00:09:55,620
by being at peace, by not doing anything, by letting all that chaos out of your head.

108
00:09:56,224 --> 00:10:05,264
And with that continuous stream of information that social media is giving you, and AI is giving you, and your agents

109
00:10:05,744 --> 00:10:09,184
are giving you, and all of that kind of stuff, there is no more

110
00:10:10,784 --> 00:10:14,864
moment to just sit there, close your eyes, and

111
00:10:17,358 --> 00:10:18,544
get to know yourself.

112
00:10:19,662 --> 00:10:25,904
What is the downside of that? If I have a question, instant satisfaction, I have my answer. I can I can go to the next one.

113
00:10:26,190 --> 00:10:26,784
Go mode.

114
00:10:26,944 --> 00:10:29,584
For me, software is has always been about speed.

115
00:10:29,774 --> 00:10:36,864
And with agents, we can accelerate quite quickly. We can also break things faster, but we can also fix that faster. So speed is still for me the name of the game.

116
00:10:37,134 --> 00:10:43,908
If I don't take the time as an individual to reflect and think on certain things of what I'm doing, what is the downside of that?

117
00:10:45,934 --> 00:10:51,104
I think the downside is uh not learning as much as we used to.

118
00:10:52,526 --> 00:10:58,916
I think you need time to internalize things, and by just having an agent spit out the code for you, you're not learning.

119
00:10:59,246 --> 00:11:07,268
And I think the people who have learned it and are now using agents will have an edge over people who have not learned it and are just using agents.

120
00:11:08,304 --> 00:11:08,624
So

121
00:11:10,254 --> 00:11:17,732
learning and learning new things and and continuously learning things is what made

122
00:11:18,446 --> 00:11:21,744
engineering and software development fun.

123
00:11:23,534 --> 00:11:25,316
Diving into a new domain,

124
00:11:26,304 --> 00:11:32,784
learning a new spec, learning a new language, building a new application on a new framework.

125
00:11:33,264 --> 00:11:36,420
there's a reason why there are so many JavaScript frameworks.

126
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:39,104
Everybody learns something from someone else.

127
00:11:39,918 --> 00:11:43,264
And the only way you get there is by taking the time to internalize it.

128
00:11:43,504 --> 00:11:43,780
Yeah.

129
00:11:44,144 --> 00:11:52,384
Does learning how to operate my agents and sub agents and all the agent artifacts with as new as new harness features come out?

130
00:11:52,718 --> 00:11:54,544
Does that count as well from your perspective?

131
00:11:54,894 --> 00:11:55,504
Yes. Yeah.

132
00:11:55,982 --> 00:12:00,932
What is the biggest learning you've had in interacting with agents, or how are you effective in that way of working?

133
00:12:02,944 --> 00:12:05,188
those are for me two different questions.

134
00:12:06,064 --> 00:12:07,664
so the first one is

135
00:12:08,464 --> 00:12:10,724
let's start with how do I work with agents?

136
00:12:12,430 --> 00:12:14,084
I now scaffold the code.

137
00:12:14,894 --> 00:12:24,464
So I still create the files in the directory structure, I put some comments in those files, I sometimes even put like class names

138
00:12:24,624 --> 00:12:27,824
or some function names or a function signature.

139
00:12:29,518 --> 00:12:31,264
and that can be very free form.

140
00:12:31,886 --> 00:12:37,892
But because I do that, I guide the agents to implementing it the way I want to have it implemented.

141
00:12:38,304 --> 00:12:48,784
because what I've noticed and learned is that agents are really good at brute forcing a solution one way, but they forget how it fits into the rest of the application. So you get code duplication,

142
00:12:49,584 --> 00:12:57,744
you get cohesion problems, you get God objects, you get all of those nasty things that we don't want in the code because it makes it less flexible.

143
00:12:57,984 --> 00:13:00,464
And so I scaffold that manually.

144
00:13:00,750 --> 00:13:05,344
And that takes me 20 minutes, 30 minutes, sometimes an hour to scaffold it.

145
00:13:05,424 --> 00:13:15,264
When I start a compiler, I write my folders, I write a Lexer, I write a little bit of the spec of the language, you know, I write some folder structures for how I want the

146
00:13:15,744 --> 00:13:20,144
intermediate representation to flow from the abstract syntax tree, all of that kind of stuff.

147
00:13:20,304 --> 00:13:30,784
And then I write a lot of examples in the language that I want to have, and then the agent is just a master at spitting out a compiler exactly for the language that I need.

148
00:13:31,150 --> 00:13:32,772
Because of the scaffolding? Yes.

149
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:33,504
Wow.

150
00:13:34,862 --> 00:13:35,364
Yes.

151
00:13:36,462 --> 00:13:38,820
And if I don't do that, so I've tried

152
00:13:39,566 --> 00:13:47,584
pure brute forcing it with agents, then you spend a month or two months building an application, and you you feel

153
00:13:47,744 --> 00:13:49,828
every time you feel that you hit a wall.

154
00:13:51,214 --> 00:14:01,488
Like they're going slower, you're not getting what you want, things start breaking. When you open up the files, you don't recognize your application anymore, you don't know what has happened.

155
00:14:01,728 --> 00:14:03,684
I've had a lot of times where

156
00:14:04,528 --> 00:14:16,368
for example, I had a deploy script, and for some reason the agent decided that we were not using the deploy script anymore, but they scaffolded a few different scripts and nobody knew what it did.

157
00:14:16,768 --> 00:14:17,248
And so

158
00:14:19,662 --> 00:14:27,088
skipping that part and being very explicit in what you want your agent to do is I think a skill that will

159
00:14:28,048 --> 00:14:28,996
make a

160
00:14:29,742 --> 00:14:34,948
10x agentic engineer, a 100x agentic engineer, being very intentional.

161
00:14:35,566 --> 00:14:44,608
I would know how to do that because I've had past experience and I have an opinion on certain project structure, but also very specifically

162
00:14:45,006 --> 00:14:49,648
in the language that I'm comfortable with, because I've had most of the hands-on experience with that.

163
00:14:50,128 --> 00:14:54,724
If I were to do something in what I've never done before, building a compiler,

164
00:14:55,470 --> 00:15:00,356
how would I build up the skill and this taste, this opinion of what is a good scaffolding?

165
00:15:01,134 --> 00:15:01,636
Yeah.

166
00:15:02,368 --> 00:15:03,716
Yeah, great question.

167
00:15:06,062 --> 00:15:08,368
well, how would you have done it before, agents?

168
00:15:08,782 --> 00:15:16,048
I I would ask other people because I at the start of a project, I never started projects. I hated that thing.

169
00:15:16,288 --> 00:15:22,788
My bulk of my work experience was in projects that had already started. So when there are conventions, I can be quite effective.

170
00:15:23,168 --> 00:15:27,088
But building up those conventions was not really a muscle that I've trained.

171
00:15:27,406 --> 00:15:32,484
So I would rely on other people where I had seen that they have the skill of building up conventions.

172
00:15:32,848 --> 00:15:36,996
And I would work together with them or ask them kind of what are things that really matter.

173
00:15:37,486 --> 00:15:44,100
I haven't really internalized those things because I haven't gone into this repetitive cycle of having to do that myself nowadays.

174
00:15:44,782 --> 00:15:47,808
If I were to do that now, I would likely ask my agent.

175
00:15:49,088 --> 00:15:51,172
Yeah. But also ask for help if I'm stuck.

176
00:15:51,630 --> 00:15:52,132
Yeah.

177
00:15:53,328 --> 00:15:53,648
Um

178
00:15:54,448 --> 00:16:02,288
how I would do it these days if I had to learn something completely new, I would first search for open source projects that are already there.

179
00:16:03,470 --> 00:16:07,172
And I would download those projects. And if you want to work within,

180
00:16:07,854 --> 00:16:15,888
I call it an agent bubble, then you would ask the agent, okay, distill the great patterns from this project, distill the great patterns from that project.

181
00:16:16,078 --> 00:16:24,448
And then I would have some sort of skill if you're using Claude or something like that, with with Grill Me or Teach Me or something like that.

182
00:16:24,608 --> 00:16:31,524
So teach me the fundamentals of these projects and and why you think these projects work or what the bad parts are of this project.

183
00:16:31,888 --> 00:16:39,748
And that would that would take you a day or two days, and then you would learn the the bad parts of the system, but also the good parts of the system.

184
00:16:40,494 --> 00:16:51,328
Uh, and then what I would do, as I always do, is I would just go outside, I would grab a cup of coffee, I would sit there staring into emptiness,

185
00:16:51,728 --> 00:16:56,128
and I would just wait until all of that stuff is internalized.

186
00:16:57,646 --> 00:17:03,652
And if I have questions, I can go back inside and ask those questions and then sit outside and internalize them again.

187
00:17:04,768 --> 00:17:11,620
And only when I had a good feeling of what I wanted to do, I would start with very small POCs,

188
00:17:12,654 --> 00:17:18,564
like a certain scaffold, and then ask the agent is this a scaffold that would work? Would that solve both problems?

189
00:17:19,648 --> 00:17:21,328
and continue in that loop.

190
00:17:21,614 --> 00:17:24,548
And once I would feel comfortable with the solution,

191
00:17:25,486 --> 00:17:26,928
I would ask the agent to build it.

192
00:17:29,232 --> 00:17:37,472
Because I've learned over the years that you need to resist that early dopamine hit of getting something immediately.

193
00:17:38,912 --> 00:17:48,324
And just from my understanding, if I don't have the scaffolding, the consequence of that is that the agent kind of interprets what is good scaffolding and just goes ahead and builds the thing.

194
00:17:48,750 --> 00:17:52,352
Like very proactively, tries to get the thing done, regardless of structure.

195
00:17:52,592 --> 00:17:52,912
Yes.

196
00:17:53,152 --> 00:17:56,352
But also regardless of what you want to have in the future.

197
00:17:57,614 --> 00:18:02,672
So it will build, it will, that's why I said it will brute force exactly what you ask it right now.

198
00:18:02,832 --> 00:18:06,592
Yeah. And that's a problem because the future is kind of malleable, or how do you see that?

199
00:18:07,246 --> 00:18:10,852
Yeah. So change is still the only consistent thing in software.

200
00:18:12,272 --> 00:18:13,872
and so if you have

201
00:18:14,752 --> 00:18:22,500
for example, in a compiler, no one writes a compiler and then sits back and says, it's done.

202
00:18:23,566 --> 00:18:24,932
It will always evolve.

203
00:18:25,326 --> 00:18:33,392
You will have a different language feature, or you will have a different way of doing optimizations or stuff like that. And

204
00:18:35,054 --> 00:18:36,672
let me give you a concrete example.

205
00:18:36,878 --> 00:18:40,420
I built an entire compiler with a very simple list system in there.

206
00:18:41,792 --> 00:18:45,060
and then I wanted that list system to expand.

207
00:18:45,646 --> 00:18:52,432
And I was working on that compiler for a month, I think, with the agent, and I was doing that in a language that I didn't understand that well.

208
00:18:53,312 --> 00:18:57,732
and so at the end, I asked the agent, okay, please review the list feature.

209
00:18:58,192 --> 00:19:00,612
And he said it was implemented five times.

210
00:19:02,606 --> 00:19:06,116
And that is the thing that happens if you just brute force it with an agent.

211
00:19:06,672 --> 00:19:12,672
And so you'll need to have iterations to clean things up again. But cleaning it up means you need to recognize what the problem is.

212
00:19:12,832 --> 00:19:13,156
Yeah.

213
00:19:13,934 --> 00:19:15,072
Yeah, for me, that's the hard part.

214
00:19:15,392 --> 00:19:26,272
I I like your approach of looking at what is out there in open source and then asking what are good patterns and really helping the agent to help you explain things or understand things better.

215
00:19:26,432 --> 00:19:31,872
So you help the agent and it needs to explain things so you can understand it better, so you can internalize.

216
00:19:32,206 --> 00:19:41,632
And likely whatever question you have, I like the functionality now that you can just go into a QA until you have reached the bottom or kind of top, depending on your mental model,

217
00:19:41,792 --> 00:19:46,532
of whatever curiosity you have and understanding you want to have before you want to get started.

218
00:19:47,406 --> 00:19:51,748
I don't do that often enough, I think, but I like this pattern and I'm probably going to take it with me.

219
00:19:52,032 --> 00:19:54,212
When I then think of okay, how would I scaffold

220
00:19:55,118 --> 00:19:56,432
if I haven't learned the language?

221
00:19:56,686 --> 00:19:58,500
Because I don't know many languages, I know you do.

222
00:19:59,118 --> 00:20:01,312
Do I do that with my agent? Is that okay?

223
00:20:01,646 --> 00:20:02,592
Can I start like that?

224
00:20:02,958 --> 00:20:03,952
Or what would your advice be?

225
00:20:05,072 --> 00:20:05,392
Yeah.

226
00:20:05,678 --> 00:20:06,992
Just start with the agent.

227
00:20:08,046 --> 00:20:19,472
But also ask the agent, okay, what are some critical ideas behind this language? And please explain this language uh to me in terms of this language that I do know.

228
00:20:20,142 --> 00:20:27,268
So, for example, if you know C sharp and you want to learn Go, just ask the agent, okay, I know C sharp.

229
00:20:27,822 --> 00:20:32,272
What do I need to know in order to be idiomatically aligned with Go?

230
00:20:32,846 --> 00:20:33,952
What are the differences?

231
00:20:34,254 --> 00:20:35,300
What am I doing wrong?

232
00:20:35,472 --> 00:20:40,644
And then scaffold a little bit of code. It doesn't have to be correct. That's the cool thing about agents these days.

233
00:20:41,038 --> 00:20:43,940
Your code doesn't have to be correct. The agent will correct it.

234
00:20:44,302 --> 00:20:53,232
But if you scaffold that code, even if it's just a quick sketch with some comments in there, and then ask the agent to review your

235
00:20:53,872 --> 00:21:04,032
scaffolding, and then the agent will do a review and will tell you honestly, usually, this is wrong, that is wrong, this is wrong, and then you start challenging.

236
00:21:05,198 --> 00:21:07,108
Does it still make sense to learn a language?

237
00:21:08,592 --> 00:21:14,020
I think we are at the brink of needing a new programming language.

238
00:21:14,926 --> 00:21:17,232
Uh and and I know everybody says that.

239
00:21:18,734 --> 00:21:24,580
But there are a few fundamental things that I need to that I think need to change in programming in general.

240
00:21:25,134 --> 00:21:28,484
The first one is I think we need to get rid of the heap.

241
00:21:29,454 --> 00:21:32,240
I think the heap just needs to go completely.

242
00:21:32,686 --> 00:21:37,636
We don't need pointers, we don't need dynamic memory, we don't need all of that stuff anymore.

243
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:43,172
So I think we need to have a very deterministic language.

244
00:21:43,502 --> 00:21:46,240
And we need to have that language in three phases.

245
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:52,100
So you need to have a language in which the human writes the code,

246
00:21:54,062 --> 00:22:04,480
which is a combination of natural language and some form of structured language, like just maybe even just a keyword component, and then the name of the component.

247
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:12,160
And then inside of that component with an indentation, you can write something like markdown to specify what that component needs to do.

248
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:20,960
And then have a sort of an intermediate phase where we generate things like a diagram or a video or just something very visual

249
00:22:21,326 --> 00:22:26,500
or audio, where the agent tries to explain what you're trying to achieve.

250
00:22:26,830 --> 00:22:34,820
And then maybe you have a layer in between where with voice or with something else, you can you can challenge it and you can you can structure it from that visual layer

251
00:22:35,120 --> 00:22:36,400
back to that first

252
00:22:38,414 --> 00:22:39,108
language.

253
00:22:39,566 --> 00:22:47,840
And I think from that we need to have a representation that runs on a machine with all of the old assumptions

254
00:22:48,174 --> 00:22:49,092
stripped away.

255
00:22:50,094 --> 00:22:51,876
And what I mean with that is

256
00:22:52,880 --> 00:22:58,720
we need bounded while statements, we need bounded loops, we need the actor models,

257
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:07,840
we need to really get rid of the heap and go back to this record structure or it used to be called a plex.

258
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:08,560
And

259
00:23:09,518 --> 00:23:19,040
we need to go back to a situation where we can reason about what is actually running on your machine instead of the non-deterministic thing that we have right now.

260
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:22,500
Is that the why? Specifically because of the non-deterministic behavior?

261
00:23:22,990 --> 00:23:24,320
Yes, but also

262
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:30,880
the performance is going to be extremely high if you have a runtime like that.

263
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:33,636
and secondly,

264
00:23:38,542 --> 00:23:41,120
our systems are a bit spread out.

265
00:23:41,326 --> 00:23:44,804
So if you want to build for a GPU, you need to write

266
00:23:46,830 --> 00:23:50,564
in a completely different style than if you write for a CPU.

267
00:23:51,182 --> 00:23:55,620
And that's because CPUs are very highly optimized for path

268
00:23:57,040 --> 00:24:03,600
finding and and and just preloading data and all of that kind of stuff in your in your ways in the CPU.

269
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:06,240
And so your GPU is

270
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:07,520
very

271
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:09,520
deterministic.

272
00:24:09,710 --> 00:24:14,080
So you just allocate that piece of memory, you fill it, you run some functions on it, and that's it.

273
00:24:14,240 --> 00:24:19,440
You don't have a heap, you don't have allocation, you don't have all of that complexity that you have on a CPU.

274
00:24:19,854 --> 00:24:20,640
Now, if you

275
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,548
it's really hard to bring the GPU up to the CPU.

276
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:27,780
and also I think it's a bad approach.

277
00:24:28,238 --> 00:24:36,480
I think we need to bring the CPU in a deterministic way back to what a GPU does, but with different computational sets on top of it.

278
00:24:37,134 --> 00:24:38,180
And that is

279
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:52,160
going to make sure that we can have more of these powers like SIMD and more of these structures like okay, you want to have some sort of graph structure. Okay, let's let's project it somewhere.

280
00:24:52,400 --> 00:24:58,820
And that is the core, I think, of what we need in the future have very deterministic languages.

281
00:25:01,678 --> 00:25:05,092
I think I forgot to say why a deterministic language is important.

282
00:25:06,848 --> 00:25:09,060
Imagine a satellite.

283
00:25:09,806 --> 00:25:11,088
You shoot it into space.

284
00:25:11,278 --> 00:25:13,168
It has a finite amount of memory.

285
00:25:13,806 --> 00:25:21,168
And if you had heap allocations, you would have out-of-memory exceptions or no pointer exceptions in your satellite.

286
00:25:22,062 --> 00:25:30,848
you don't want that. And we're going more to robotics and satellites and machine learning and all of that kind of stuff. And you want that to be as deterministic as possible.

287
00:25:31,088 --> 00:25:37,700
If you have two gigabytes of memory, you want to know exactly that the application is never going to flow out of that.

288
00:25:38,702 --> 00:25:43,488
So that's why we don't we we cannot use dynamic allocations anymore, I think.

289
00:25:43,982 --> 00:25:48,388
I feel like that's why, at least when we're talking about satellites, people are building a lot of things in Rust.

290
00:25:49,134 --> 00:25:57,968
In essence, because I'm expecting a lot of those would be caught early, as early as possible, so they don't happen when it actually runs in space.

291
00:25:59,408 --> 00:26:07,648
Rust has a standard on top of it with a very reduced set of language features you can use to make the language deterministic.

292
00:26:08,448 --> 00:26:13,568
But it is a true pain to write, or at least has been my experience.

293
00:26:13,968 --> 00:26:14,448
By hand.

294
00:26:14,766 --> 00:26:17,488
Yes. Have you experimented with letting agents write Rust?

295
00:26:17,728 --> 00:26:18,048
Yes.

296
00:26:18,478 --> 00:26:19,008
How was that?

297
00:26:19,488 --> 00:26:22,852
It was slower than most other languages,

298
00:26:23,968 --> 00:26:24,288
but

299
00:26:25,198 --> 00:26:27,008
it was kind of fun.

300
00:26:27,822 --> 00:26:29,604
So the agents are quite good at it.

301
00:26:29,870 --> 00:26:34,308
They don't split up the code the way I would like them to split the code.

302
00:26:34,734 --> 00:26:39,568
And I've always found Rust very hard to change later.

303
00:26:40,048 --> 00:26:43,396
So if you have a piece of Rust code that works,

304
00:26:45,966 --> 00:26:48,100
I prefer not to touch it anymore.

305
00:26:48,688 --> 00:26:49,408
It's done.

306
00:26:49,728 --> 00:26:50,288
Yeah. Yeah.

307
00:26:50,606 --> 00:26:54,404
Yeah. And in other languages, it's much easier to start refactoring.

308
00:26:55,408 --> 00:26:57,488
but that's my personal experience of Rust.

309
00:26:58,542 --> 00:27:08,448
I don't want to discourage anybody from trying the language because it's a beautiful language and it has a lot of features that you would actually want from a modern language,

310
00:27:08,528 --> 00:27:10,288
like discriminated unions

311
00:27:11,248 --> 00:27:12,608
and all of that kind of stuff.

312
00:27:12,768 --> 00:27:15,168
But the borrow checker by itself

313
00:27:16,270 --> 00:27:18,528
is a way to manage heap data.

314
00:27:20,334 --> 00:27:23,652
And if you did not have a heap, you would not need a borrow checker.

315
00:27:24,622 --> 00:27:31,524
So just imagine that if you have a record and you lay that out in memory, and the only thing you have is the start index of that record,

316
00:27:31,790 --> 00:27:38,148
and that record will always be there because at the start of your application, all of your data is already laid out in memory.

317
00:27:38,528 --> 00:27:44,356
Now you have the start index, and now you only have offsets for all of the fields in your record.

318
00:27:45,518 --> 00:27:45,988
That's

319
00:27:46,862 --> 00:27:47,728
blazingly fast.

320
00:27:48,942 --> 00:27:49,572
Interesting.

321
00:27:50,510 --> 00:27:53,168
This new language, will it be

322
00:27:53,966 --> 00:27:54,768
one language?

323
00:27:55,150 --> 00:27:55,648
Do you think?

324
00:27:56,270 --> 00:27:57,348
Will we converge?

325
00:28:01,088 --> 00:28:06,048
my opinion in that is that's because that's why I have those three layers. Yeah.

326
00:28:06,288 --> 00:28:06,528
Yeah.

327
00:28:06,928 --> 00:28:08,260
I think the resulting

328
00:28:09,454 --> 00:28:13,008
runtime and and resulting code is going to be identical.

329
00:28:16,302 --> 00:28:17,668
But the layers in between

330
00:28:19,118 --> 00:28:21,088
are going to be more or less plug and play.

331
00:28:21,568 --> 00:28:22,148
Interesting.

332
00:28:22,606 --> 00:28:24,048
I think it will

333
00:28:25,408 --> 00:28:33,968
go so far that everybody, just by having a conversation with their agent, will define their own language on top of that?

334
00:28:35,168 --> 00:28:43,328
The problem that I sometimes have, at least with helping my agents be more effective, is, and this is a problem across humans as well,

335
00:28:43,488 --> 00:28:47,396
across teams and across different hierarchies, speaking the same language.

336
00:28:47,918 --> 00:28:49,408
When I say component, what does that mean?

337
00:28:49,582 --> 00:28:58,848
When I'm talking about an agent, is that a custom agent? Is that a local agent? Is that subagent, main agent, I don't know, any type of other agents that people have in their mind?

338
00:28:59,008 --> 00:29:01,124
Speaking the same language has always been an issue.

339
00:29:01,646 --> 00:29:07,972
So then with this newer way of working, and you mentioning people will define kind of their own language and interaction,

340
00:29:08,622 --> 00:29:12,228
how would people still or how would kind of this shared language?

341
00:29:15,214 --> 00:29:16,928
how would people create this shared language?

342
00:29:17,168 --> 00:29:17,540
Yeah.

343
00:29:18,542 --> 00:29:21,572
That is that is the million dollar question, I think.

344
00:29:22,862 --> 00:29:25,008
My personal view on that is

345
00:29:31,214 --> 00:29:32,864
If I go back 20 years

346
00:29:33,838 --> 00:29:36,260
and I would build a CRM system,

347
00:29:38,094 --> 00:29:42,544
there was no discussion about what a customer is, because the customer is that schema.

348
00:29:43,984 --> 00:29:46,864
How you get to that schema, that is difficult.

349
00:29:46,944 --> 00:29:49,892
And how that schema evolves, that is difficult.

350
00:29:50,464 --> 00:29:52,064
But the now,

351
00:29:52,944 --> 00:29:54,500
that is the definition of a customer.

352
00:29:55,584 --> 00:29:55,904
So

353
00:29:56,784 --> 00:30:02,692
the hardest part is to come to a definition and start iterating from there.

354
00:30:03,726 --> 00:30:12,624
So I think if I understand the question correctly, my assumption will be that we need to start much simpler than what we do right now.

355
00:30:13,166 --> 00:30:15,812
Even if a customer is just an ID,

356
00:30:17,104 --> 00:30:17,424
then

357
00:30:19,024 --> 00:30:22,144
I think you will find that you have immediate consensus.

358
00:30:22,318 --> 00:30:24,484
Yes, of course, a customer needs an ID.

359
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:27,588
And then we start iterating.

360
00:30:28,784 --> 00:30:29,104
And

361
00:30:30,144 --> 00:30:33,824
so data has given us a lot of patterns.

362
00:30:33,984 --> 00:30:42,624
You can have a star model, you can have like a sales customer, a marketing customer, you can have all of these kinds of different things, and you can have different types of models.

363
00:30:43,024 --> 00:30:45,584
but the core thing is that

364
00:30:47,054 --> 00:30:49,264
the agreement is not a

365
00:30:50,350 --> 00:30:56,804
fixed point in time, but the agreement should be over the evolution of what you're trying to build.

366
00:30:58,224 --> 00:31:05,700
And because software development was a lot slower, people were focusing more on right now than on the evolution.

367
00:31:06,126 --> 00:31:08,304
It's also why uh,

368
00:31:09,486 --> 00:31:14,820
in my opinion, agile is is no longer agile how it should be.

369
00:31:15,118 --> 00:31:16,784
We've talked about this multiple times.

370
00:31:16,910 --> 00:31:19,684
Yeah, I think agile got a little bit captured.

371
00:31:20,064 --> 00:31:27,904
And now with agents, we can get back to this idea of okay, start here, let's evolve fast, and let's do it.

372
00:31:29,966 --> 00:31:30,820
Very interesting.

373
00:31:31,278 --> 00:31:39,748
In our whole conversation and in how you approach kind of agentic engineering, I didn't expect the scaffolding to be kind of this differentiating factor.

374
00:31:40,078 --> 00:31:45,984
And mainly my thought comes from, at least in my bubble, there's some people that say, I haven't looked at the code in a while, right?

375
00:31:46,064 --> 00:31:50,308
The way I am effective is I let my agent handle everything, I don't look at the code.

376
00:31:50,702 --> 00:31:58,624
And in the end, sometimes I review or other people review my code that the agent has created, or I let multiple agents do kind of an adversarial review,

377
00:31:58,784 --> 00:31:59,584
blind review.

378
00:31:59,854 --> 00:32:05,904
They found way of working and they're trying to nudge their behavior in such a way that they are effective without looking at the code.

379
00:32:06,126 --> 00:32:08,004
Yeah, but that's not your approach. No.

380
00:32:08,590 --> 00:32:12,384
I I also honestly I don't know what the fun of that would be.

381
00:32:13,326 --> 00:32:23,108
Unless you're building a product that you're going to be selling and you're making money off of that product and you need to get it out as fast as possible,

382
00:32:23,566 --> 00:32:25,444
uh then I understand it.

383
00:32:26,030 --> 00:32:28,740
But if it is purely for yourself,

384
00:32:29,486 --> 00:32:37,584
what's I I don't find I don't understand the fun in asking the agent to do something and then

385
00:32:39,214 --> 00:32:44,932
go out and do some shopping or go to the cinema and then coming back and then being satisfied with what the agent gave?

386
00:32:45,344 --> 00:32:49,584
Maybe if I would talk to like about myself, I would want the outcome.

387
00:32:49,744 --> 00:32:57,904
I wouldn't necessarily care about how to achieve the outcome in what the code looks like or is this maintainable, but I would want the result of that outcome specifically.

388
00:32:58,144 --> 00:32:58,404
Yeah.

389
00:32:59,104 --> 00:33:05,744
And then how does that fit with what you said a few moments ago with um not finishing the pro the project?

390
00:33:06,318 --> 00:33:06,544
Yeah.

391
00:33:06,862 --> 00:33:07,748
Sometimes I don't.

392
00:33:08,654 --> 00:33:12,484
I get to that outcome specifically. Sometimes I have a thought of how would I solve this?

393
00:33:12,846 --> 00:33:15,616
And then I let the agents reason over it overnight.

394
00:33:15,918 --> 00:33:20,336
And I feel like this behavior is also stimulated by the way I get weekly credits.

395
00:33:20,686 --> 00:33:25,296
And I have this FOMO feeling of if I don't use my credits, I'm kind of wasting what I'm paying for.

396
00:33:25,536 --> 00:33:30,756
So I want to maximize what I get, especially now because I think inference might get more expensive.

397
00:33:31,776 --> 00:33:32,068
But

398
00:33:32,910 --> 00:33:38,372
with that, I have the behavior of let me just throw an agent at this problem and see what comes out of it.

399
00:33:38,766 --> 00:33:41,856
And then I do explore and kind of look into what was there.

400
00:33:42,222 --> 00:33:50,896
And sometimes I don't reach the outcome because I at least have some form of learning or some form of kind of having orchestrated my behavior and taking away something from that.

401
00:33:51,216 --> 00:33:53,060
That's currently my way of working.

402
00:33:53,582 --> 00:34:00,740
But for example, when I had one day of just innovation budget where we can spend one day on whatever, I built a harness that builds games.

403
00:34:01,166 --> 00:34:03,108
And I saw this from Google.

404
00:34:03,566 --> 00:34:10,976
I was at a conference and I was like, are you guys going to open source this? Because I would love to try it out with whomever, basically. I have a little brother that's 16 years younger than me.

405
00:34:11,136 --> 00:34:18,884
I think he would love building a game, but he doesn't know how to program. I think if I could give him this kind of harness and he can do speech to text, he would build games.

406
00:34:19,438 --> 00:34:20,496
But they didn't open source it.

407
00:34:20,654 --> 00:34:22,816
So then I thought, ah, I'll just build it.

408
00:34:23,150 --> 00:34:28,036
So that's what I did. So in the end, my outcome was I have a harness. I don't necessarily care.

409
00:34:28,590 --> 00:34:39,056
Or I did go into some details of kind of what I want in terms of outcome in the game, the style, maybe a templating engine. I wanted determinism. That's really what I focused my time and effort on.

410
00:34:39,470 --> 00:34:48,772
If someone creates a game in certain functionality, I want to make sure that whatever is the outcome of that game, built through the harness that I built, is close to what the user is expecting.

411
00:34:49,358 --> 00:34:57,316
And then my brother built a game and then he really deepened out that one game and then he built more games. And I gave it to my partner and people that are non-technical and they built games.

412
00:34:57,998 --> 00:35:02,916
But I didn't really look at the code. That was the outcome that I wanted. Yeah, but that's perfect, right? Yeah.

413
00:35:03,534 --> 00:35:04,356
That's perfect.

414
00:35:04,782 --> 00:35:12,416
Because the intention was not to learn how to build games or how to build a game engine or that kind of stuff, but the goal was to make people happy.

415
00:35:13,358 --> 00:35:15,652
And that is perfectly good.

416
00:35:16,750 --> 00:35:18,416
But then you have that objective.

417
00:35:18,702 --> 00:35:23,376
That objective was to get a smile on the faces of the people you enjoy seeing smiling.

418
00:35:24,416 --> 00:35:25,764
